1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,058
Book Three, Proposition Eight.

2
00:00:02,058 --> 00:00:32,359
If a point be taken outside a circle and from the point segments be drawn through to the circle, one of which is through the center and the others at random, then of the segments falling on the concave arc, that through the center is greatest and the nearer to it is always greater than the more remote; while of those falling on the convex arc, that between the point and the diameter is least, the nearer to the least is always less than the more remote, and only two equal segments will fall on the circle from the point, one on each side of the least.

3
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:40,330
Let A B C be a circle and D an exterior point, with secants D A through the center and D E, D F, and D C meeting the convex arc at G, K, L, and H.

4
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:50,392
Take the center M of the circle A B C by Three One, and join the radii M E, M F, M C, M K, M L, and M H.

5
00:00:50,392 --> 00:00:59,200
Then on M D at M, construct angle D M B equal to angle K M D by One Twenty-Three, and join D B.

6
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:07,029
On the concave arc, radius A M equals E M, so D A equals E M plus M D, which exceeds D E by One Twenty.

7
00:01:07,029 --> 00:01:20,410
Since radii M E and M F are equal, M D is common, and angle E M D exceeds angle F M D, the Hinge Theorem, One Twenty-Four, gives D E greater than D F, and similarly D F greater than D C.

8
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:30,409
On the convex arc, in triangle M K D, M K plus K D exceeds M D by One Twenty; subtracting equal radii M K and M G leaves D G less than D K.

9
00:01:30,409 --> 00:01:44,148
And because M K and K D meet inside triangle M L D, their sum is less than M L plus L D by One Twenty-One; subtracting equal radii M K and M L leaves D K less than D L, and D L less than D H.

10
00:01:44,148 --> 00:01:49,328
By One Four, triangles M K D and M B D are congruent, so D K equals D B.

11
00:01:49,328 --> 00:01:56,953
Any third equal segment D N would force D B to equal D N—nearer equal to more remote, which is impossible.

12
00:01:56,953 --> 00:02:09,902
Therefore, D A is greatest on the concave arc, D G is least on the convex arc, distances vary monotonically with inclination, and only two equal segments fall from D on the circle.

13
00:02:09,902 --> 00:02:11,700
As was required to prove.

14
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,970
Proposition Three Eight is the exterior counterpart to Three Seven.

15
00:02:15,970 --> 00:02:21,315
Together they govern every secant and distance drawn to a circle from any point in the plane.

16
00:02:21,315 --> 00:02:28,900
Notably, Euclid deploys Proposition One Twenty-One—the interior triangle inequality—to order the segments falling on the convex arc.

17
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:38,440
